{
  "abstract": "A machine-checked proof of the Eastin-Knill theorem: a quantum code of dimension at least two that detects every error on a single subsystem has no universal set of transversal logical gates. The system has finitely many sites of any finite dimensions; a code is an orthogonal projector P; it detects single-site errors when P E P is a multiple of P for every operator E on one site; a transversal gate is a tensor product of one unitary per site, and it is logical when it commutes with P. Up to a scalar on the code there are only finitely many logical transversal gates; if the code has dimension at least two they are not universal, since some logical unitary differs on the code by a fixed distance from every logical transversal gate times every scalar. The same holds for transversal gates across several code blocks (Eastin and Knill's Corollary 1). The proof avoids Lie theory: near the identity a transversal gate is exp(i H) with H a sum of single-site operators, error detection makes it act on the code as a scalar, so the gates acting as scalars form an open subgroup of a compact group, which has finite index. A three-qutrit code is constructed to show the hypotheses can be met.",
