    "related_formalizations": [
      {
        "identifier": "https://github.com/xh-xihe/sslp-classification/blob/6b5249a620b8cb451e4fec4a675be72182356e3d/MainTheory/EastinKnill.lean",
        "note": "An earlier Lean proof of the theorem, MainTheory.EastinKnill.eastin_knill (file first committed 6 September 2026, Lean 4.27.0), inside a project on transversal diagonal gates. A code is an isometric encoding V of dimension at least two, on sites of any finite dimensions, detecting every operator on one site; the conclusion is that the transversal logical gates are not dense, up to phase, in the logical unitary group. Its route differs: every transversal logical gate has a power of uniformly bounded order acting on the code as a scalar (each site diagonalised and embedded into qubits), so up to phase there are finitely many. The author learned of it from a review after this proof was written; nothing is taken from it (no licence stated). This entry adds Corollary 1 for several code blocks, non-universality as an explicit distance on the code up to every scalar, the equivalence with the paper's definition of a logical gate, the correction to the paper's phase constant, a route through compactness, and a Comparator challenge.",
        "relationship": "independent"
      },
      {
        "identifier": "https://github.com/paulklemstine/Lean/blob/59eaf267ab7a3ffeecb27916f59965b6d8c8182e/Catalog/Computation/EastinKnillLie.lean",
        "note": "Proves, for an abstract matrix A with A P = P A P and P A P = c P, that exp(t A) acts on the code as a phase: the infinitesimal step in abstract form. There is no tensor-product structure, no finiteness and no non-universality theorem. Here the corresponding fact is derived from the gate being logical rather than assumed. No licence stated.",
        "relationship": "independent"
      },
      {
        "identifier": "https://github.com/tomdif/unifiedtheory/blob/532e9c28ca9b1e8d29ed82bc5b6c0918778bf3a4/UnifiedTheory/LayerC/EastinKnill.lean",
        "note": "Named after the theorem; proves that a finite set of 2x2 matrices misses some phase gate. Finiteness of the transversal group appears only as an unproved Prop. No licence stated.",
        "relationship": "independent"
      },
      {
        "identifier": "https://github.com/ceccec/millennium-solutions/blob/b4524b9c794f6775b636d66efb1d6b2c12459cb4/src/iso/iec-4879-quantum/quantum_textbooks.lean",
        "note": "textbook_eastin_knill decides that the group generated by H and S has 24 elements up to phase; not a statement of the theorem.",
        "relationship": "independent"
      },
      {
        "identifier": "https://github.com/zblore/knill-laflamme-lean",
        "note": "The author's Lean proof of the Knill-Laflamme theorem (Palomar PALOMAR-2026-11-15-000005). The detection hypothesis here is that every operator on one site compresses to a scalar on the code, P E P = c P (equivalently, the code corrects the erasure of any one known site), stated directly; no code is shared, and the Knill-Laflamme theorem itself is not used.",
        "relationship": "other"
      },
      {
        "identifier": "https://github.com/ShivaneDadi05/CMPSC_292C_Final",
        "note": "A course project (November 2026) whose README mentions the Eastin-Knill theorem; its only Lean file, pauli_foundations.lean, proves facts about Pauli matrices and contains no statement of the theorem. No licence stated.",
        "relationship": "independent"
      },
